"c3","label":"A Neglected Corner Becomes a Welcoming Garden"}],"answer":"c3","answerMode":"exact","requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"itemSnapshot":{"id":"lp3.main_idea.l2.A.best_title.v1","contentVersion":"literacy-mock-v1","skillId":"main_idea","domainId":"reading","mapDomainId":"comprehension","level":2,"format":"choice","answerMode":"exact","prompt":"Which title fits this passage best?","passage":"Nobody used the muddy corner of the school garden. Water gathered there, and wrappers caught against its broken fence. A class first cleared the rubbish and repaired the fence. They mixed compost into the ground so water drained better. Then they planted sunflowers where children could watch them grow. By summer, yellow flowers stood above the old muddy patch. The class added a bench for anyone passing through. People now stopped there instead of hurrying past the mess.","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"Repairing the Fence Around a Muddy Corner"},{"id":"c1","label":"Growing Flowers to Sell at a Market"},{"id":"c2","label":"A Flower Garden Is Closed to Visitors"},{"id":"c3","label":"A Neglected Corner Becomes a Welcoming Garden"}],"answer":"c3","targetWord":"","renderTargetText":false,"image":"","imageAlt":"","modality":"reading","displayPassageDuringResponse":true,"hideWrittenLabels":false,"tutorialOnly":false,"requiredAudioCues":[{"role":"instruction","text":"Which title fits this passage best?","path":"/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"}],"requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"stimulusKey":"nobody used the muddy corner of the school garden. water gathered there, and wrappers caught against its broken fence. a class first cleared the rubbish and repaired the fence. they mixed compost into the ground so water drained better. then they planted sunflowers where children could watch them grow. by summer, yellow flowers stood above the old muddy patch. the class added a bench for anyone passing through. people now stopped there instead of hurrying past the mess.","constructClaim":"best_title","sourceItemId":"lp3.main_idea.l2.A.best_title.v1","sourceFormat":"COMPREHENSION","sourceProvenance":{"generatedBy":"assessment-rebuild-gate","sourceFile":"tools/assessmentRebuild/authoring/main_idea.mjs","wave":"","standardVersion":"v3"},"mediaDecision":{"itemId":"lp3.main_idea.l2.A.best_title.v1","role":"text-only","paths":[],"constructReview":"approved","answerNeutral":"approved-empty-setting"},"distractorRationales":{"Growing Flowers to Sell at a Market":"D-TOPIC-ADJACENT","A Flower Garden Is Closed to Visitors":"D-OPPOSITE","Repairing the Fence Around a Muddy Corner":"D-DETAIL-AS-MAIN"},"requiredImagePaths":[],"mediaReady":true}},{"id":"lp3.main_idea.l2.B.best_title.v2","skillId":"main_idea","domainId":"reading","level":2,"format":"choice","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"The Best Ways to Repair Old Footbridges"},{"id":"c1","label":"A Dangerous Route That Families Must Abandon"},{"id":"c2","label":"Finding Good Things on an Unwanted Detour"},{"id":"c3","label":"A Heron Hunts Beside the School Path"}],"answer":"c2","answerMode":"exact","requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"itemSnapshot":{"id":"lp3.main_idea.l2.B.best_title.v2","contentVersion":"literacy-mock-v1","skillId":"main_idea","domainId":"reading","mapDomainId":"comprehension","level":2,"format":"choice","answerMode":"exact","prompt":"Which title fits this passage best?","passage":"The old footbridge closed while workers replaced its damaged boards. Families had to follow a longer path beside the stream. At first, everyone complained about the extra walk to school. Then children noticed blackberries growing beside a hollow tree. A heron stood in the shallows, waiting for a fish. They began leaving early so they could watch it hunting. When the repaired bridge opened, the short route was safe. Some families still chose the longer path on quiet mornings.","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"The Best Ways to Repair Old Footbridges"},{"id":"c1","label":"A Dangerous Route That Families Must Abandon"},{"id":"c2","label":"Finding Good Things on an Unwanted Detour"},{"id":"c3","label":"A Heron Hunts Beside the School Path"}],"answer":"c2","targetWord":"","renderTargetText":false,"image":"","imageAlt":"","modality":"reading","displayPassageDuringResponse":true,"hideWrittenLabels":false,"tutorialOnly":false,"requiredAudioCues":[{"role":"instruction","text":"Which title fits this passage best?","path":"/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"}],"requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"stimulusKey":"the old footbridge closed while workers replaced its damaged boards. families had to follow a longer path beside the stream. at first, everyone complained about the extra walk to school. then children noticed blackberries growing beside a hollow tree. a heron stood in the shallows, waiting for a fish. they began leaving early so they could watch it hunting. when the repaired bridge opened, the short route was safe. some families still chose the longer path on quiet mornings.","constructClaim":"best_title","sourceItemId":"lp3.main_idea.l2.B.best_title.v2","sourceFormat":"COMPREHENSION","sourceProvenance":{"generatedBy":"assessment-rebuild-gate","sourceFile":"tools/assessmentRebuild/authoring/main_idea.mjs","wave":"","standardVersion":"v3"},"mediaDecision":{"itemId":"lp3.main_idea.l2.B.best_title.v2","role":"text-only","paths":[],"constructReview":"approved","answerNeutral":"approved-empty-setting"},"distractorRationales":{"The Best Ways to Repair Old Footbridges":"D-TOPIC-ADJACENT","A Dangerous Route That Families Must Abandon":"D-OPPOSITE","A Heron Hunts Beside the School Path":"D-DETAIL-AS-MAIN"},"requiredImagePaths":[],"mediaReady":true}},{"id":"lp3.main_idea.l2.C.best_title.v3","skillId":"main_idea","domainId":"reading","level":2,"format":"choice","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"Choosing an Easier Tune for the Concert"},{"id":"c1","label":"Slowing Down to Master a Fast Rhythm"},{"id":"c2","label":"Practicing the Middle Beats of a Tune"},{"id":"c3","label":"Making Drums Louder for a Large Audience"}],"answer":"c1","answerMode":"exact","requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"itemSnapshot":{"id":"lp3.main_idea.l2.C.best_title.v3","contentVersion":"literacy-mock-v1","skillId":"main_idea","domainId":"reading","mapDomainId":"comprehension","level":2,"format":"choice","answerMode":"exact","prompt":"Which title fits this passage best?","passage":"Asha wanted to play a fast rhythm at the concert. Each evening, her sticks missed the same beats halfway through. She tried hitting harder, but the mistake stayed the same. Her teacher asked her to play each part very slowly. Asha disliked how plain the slow rhythm sounded at first. Soon she could hear exactly where her hands lost time. She joined the parts, then carefully increased her speed. At the concert, she played the whole rhythm without stumbling.","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"Choosing an Easier Tune for the Concert"},{"id":"c1","label":"Slowing Down to Master a Fast Rhythm"},{"id":"c2","label":"Practicing the Middle Beats of a Tune"},{"id":"c3","label":"Making Drums Louder for a Large Audience"}],"answer":"c1","targetWord":"","renderTargetText":false,"image":"","imageAlt":"","modality":"reading","displayPassageDuringResponse":true,"hideWrittenLabels":false,"tutorialOnly":false,"requiredAudioCues":[{"role":"instruction","text":"Which title fits this passage best?","path":"/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"}],"requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"stimulusKey":"asha wanted to play a fast rhythm at the concert. each evening, her sticks missed the same beats halfway through. she tried hitting harder, but the mistake stayed the same. her teacher asked her to play each part very slowly. asha disliked how plain the slow rhythm sounded at first. soon she could hear exactly where her hands lost time. she joined the parts, then carefully increased her speed. at the concert, she played the whole rhythm without stumbling.","constructClaim":"best_title","sourceItemId":"lp3.main_idea.l2.C.best_title.v3","sourceFormat":"COMPREHENSION","sourceProvenance":{"generatedBy":"assessment-rebuild-gate","sourceFile":"tools/assessmentRebuild/authoring/main_idea.mjs","wave":"","standardVersion":"v3"},"mediaDecision":{"itemId":"lp3.main_idea.l2.C.best_title.v3","role":"text-only","paths":[],"constructReview":"approved","answerNeutral":"approved-empty-setting"},"distractorRationales":{"Making Drums Louder for a Large Audience":"D-TOPIC-ADJACENT","Choosing an Easier Tune for the Concert":"D-OPPOSITE","Practicing the Middle Beats of a Tune":"D-DETAIL-AS-MAIN"},"requiredImagePaths":[],"mediaReady":true}},{"id":"lp3.main_idea.l2.A.best_title.v4","skillId":"main_idea","domainId":"reading","level":2,"format":"choice","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"A Safer Home That Keeps Otters Busy"},{"id":"c1","label":"Cameras Record Otters Building Steps from Stones"},{"id":"c2","label":"Training Otters to Return When Keepers Call"},{"id":"c3","label":"Removing Every Stone to Stop Otters Playing"}],"answer":"c0","answerMode":"exact","requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"itemSnapshot":{"id":"lp3.main_idea.l2.A.best_title.v4","contentVersion":"literacy-mock-v1","skillId":"main_idea","domainId":"reading","mapDomainId":"comprehension","level":2,"format":"choice","answerMode":"exact","prompt":"Which title fits this passage best?","passage":"The aquarium''s otters kept climbing out of their small pool. Cameras showed them stacking stones against the glass each night. The keepers could have removed every stone from the pool. Instead, they watched how much the animals enjoyed moving things. They built a larger space with tunnels and deeper water. Safe stones remained, but the outer walls were much higher. The otters explored, carried stones, and swam through the tunnels. They stopped escaping while keeping activities that interested them.","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"A Safer Home That Keeps Otters Busy"},{"id":"c1","label":"Cameras Record Otters Building Steps from Stones"},{"id":"c2","label":"Training Otters to Return When Keepers Call"},{"id":"c3","label":"Removing Every Stone to Stop Otters Playing"}],"answer":"c0","targetWord":"","renderTargetText":false,"image":"","imageAlt":"","modality":"reading","displayPassageDuringResponse":true,"hideWrittenLabels":false,"tutorialOnly":false,"requiredAudioCues":[{"role":"instruction","text":"Which title fits this passage best?","path":"/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"}],"requiredAudioPaths":["/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-title-fits-this-passage-best-66fdc65e69.mp3"],"stimulusKey":"the aquarium''s otters kept climbing out of their small pool. cameras showed them stacking stones against the glass each night. the keepers could have removed every stone from the pool. instead, they watched how much the animals enjoyed moving things. they built a larger space with tunnels and deeper water. safe stones remained, but the outer walls were much higher. the otters explored, carried stones, and swam through the tunnels. they stopped escaping while keeping activities that interested them.","constructClaim":"best_title","sourceItemId":"lp3.main_idea.l2.A.best_title.v4","sourceFormat":"COMPREHENSION","sourceProvenance":{"generatedBy":"assessment-rebuild-gate","sourceFile":"tools/assessmentRebuild/authori