 original objects remained protected behind their glass display cases.","choices":[{"id":"c0","label":"Removing Every Glass Case So Originals Can Be Handled","audioPath":"/audio/production/en-US/isolated_word/removing-every-glass-case-so-originals-can-be-handled-0f612b35a2.mp3"},{"id":"c1","label":"Touching Copies to Explore Museum Objects","audioPath":"/audio/production/en-US/isolated_word/touching-copies-to-explore-museum-objects-fa9d39a468.mp3"},{"id":"c2","label":"Protecting Original Bowls Behind Glass Cases","audioPath":"/audio/production/en-US/isolated_word/protecting-original-bowls-behind-glass-cases-0c582fd67e.mp3"},{"id":"c3","label":"Teaching Visitors to Make Their Own Ancient Objects","audioPath":"/audio/production/en-US/isolated_word/teaching-visitors-to-make-their-own-ancient-objects-d30ba3866e.mp3"}],"answer":"c1","targetWord":"","renderTargetText":false,"image":"","imageAlt":"","modality":"listening","displayPassageDuringResponse":false,"hideWrittenLabels":false,"tutorialOnly":false,"requiredAudioCues":[{"role":"passage","text":"Most museum objects stood behind glass that visitors could not touch. A visitor who could not see asked about a carved bowl. Spoken descriptions helped, but did not explain every curve clearly. Staff made a strong copy that people could explore by hand. They added copies of several other objects with different shapes. Labels and recordings explained which details matched the originals. Sighted visitors also noticed things while holding the carefully made copies. The original objects remained protected behind their glass display cases.","path":"/audio/production/en-US/assessment_passage/most-museum-objects-stood-behind-glass-that-visitors-could-not-touch-a-vis-4400444e7a.mp3"},{"role":"instruction","text":"Which title fits the whole passage?","path":"/audio/production/en-US/assessment_prompt/which-title-fits-the-whole-passage-7899ad9f35.mp3"},{"role":"choice","text":"Removing Every Glass Case So Originals Can Be Handled","value